1.6  /dev and udev

TL;DR
  - /dev holds device files: /dev/sda (a disk), /dev/ttyUSB0 (a serial adapter),
    /dev/null. Programs open them like files to talk to drivers.
  - udev names them when hardware appears, sets their owner and rights, and runs
    actions, following rules.
  - /dev/disk/by-id/ and /dev/disk/by-uuid/ hold names that stay the same from
    one boot to the next.

      ls -l /dev/disk/by-id/
      udevadm monitor          # plug something in, watch the events
      udevadm info /dev/sda

On your machine
  Local     70-keyboard.rules
  Packaged  2 rules files

Pitfalls
  - Your own rules go in /etc/udev/rules.d/, named like 70-keyboard.rules. A
    file there with the same name as a packaged rule replaces it.
  - After editing a rule, run sudo udevadm control --reload, then unplug and
    plug the device back (or sudo udevadm trigger).
  - "Permission denied" on a serial port or a USB device: join its group
    (dialout or uucp, plugdev) instead of a chmod 666 that udev undoes at the
    next plug.
  - /dev/sda and /dev/sdb can swap places: scripts should use /dev/disk/by-id/.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 1.6 --why

Related
  Commands  udevadm, lsblk
  Paths     /dev, /dev/disk/by-id/, /etc/udev/rules.d/
  Hub       idk disk Disk
  See also  idk 1.5 fstab and mount options
  See also  idk 10.2 Kernel modules
